// =====================================================================
// FILE: firestore.rules
// Paste into Firebase console → Firestore Database → Rules → Publish (after writing it).
// This file is NOT used by the React app; it's a record of your database rules.
// =====================================================================
//
// LESSON 1 — Firestore has no tables, it has COLLECTIONS of DOCUMENTS
//   collection "messages"            ≈ a table
//     document "aB3x..." (auto id)   ≈ a row
//       { content: "hi", userId: "...", userEmail: "...", createdAt: <timestamp> }
//   You don't create the collection beforehand: it appears the first time
//   your app adds a document to it. So there's no "create table" step.
//
// LESSON 2 — Security Rules
//   Your Firebase config is public, so ANYONE could talk to your database.
//   Rules decide who may read/write which documents.
//   If you created the database in "production mode", everything is
//   blocked until you write rules.
//
//   rules_version = '2';
//   service cloud.firestore {
//     match /databases/{database}/documents {
//
//       match /notes/{noteId} {                       // every doc in "notes"
//         allow read: if request.auth != null;        // only logged-in users
//
//         allow create: if request.auth != null
//           && request.resource.data.ownerId == request.auth.uid;  // can't pretend to be someone else
//
//         allow update, delete: if false;             // nobody can edit/delete
//       }
//     }
//   }
//
//   request.auth            = the logged-in user (null if logged out)
//   request.auth.uid        = their id
//   request.resource.data   = the document they are trying to save
//
// LESSON 3 — Realtime
//   Nothing to switch on! Every Firestore query can be "listened to" with
//   onSnapshot (see ChatRoom.jsx). Rules apply to listeners too: you only
//   receive documents your `read` rule allows.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------
// YOUR VERSION: same shape for a collection called `messages`. Documents have
//   content (string), userId (string), userEmail (string), createdAt (timestamp)
//   - read: logged-in users only
//   - create: logged-in AND request.resource.data.userId == request.auth.uid
//     (bonus: content is a string, size() > 0 and <= 1000)
//   - update, delete: nobody
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------

